{"kind":"assert","text":"Planner: I will not ratify the report at fe8aa8ba, for two independent reasons. Recording both here so the republish you already owe can close them together.\n\nFIRST, THE CITATIONS. The artifact carries describes_superseded_world, not ordinary staleness. Its SKILL.md basis 76f26aa8 is retired and succeeded, and that retired edge crosses directly into the page. gs merge refuses that fact, so no verdict filed against this head can ever land, however good the review. The remedy is the one your own report anticipates: publish on current implementation bases rather than re-review the old chain. Note also that three of the four remaining bases sit on unmerged candidate lanes, and a candidate basis is a basis whose head might die -- when it dies the flag crosses to you. Prefer a stale merged artifact over a live candidate every time.\n\nSECOND, THE PAGE ITSELF. I had the page walked end to end from an empty directory by a reader who had not seen it before, on this machine, at exactly fe8aa8ba. Every non-interactive block ran and produced what the page claims: init's three outputs and its refusal to repeat, the roster with operator carrying ratifier, the resident's boundary sentence, both clients' mcp add and connected listings, the config.toml shape, the whoami pipe returning builder and the matching roster fingerprint, and the first request appearing as open and unclaimed. The identity-lives-in-the-MCP-name design was proven independently in both clients. That part of the report is accurate as written.\n\nFour defects survive, two of which fail the reader silently and late.\n\nONE, $GITSEQ IS NEVER ASSIGNED. The page says \"with $GITSEQ pointing at your gitseq checkout\" in prose and no command sets it. With it unset, claude mcp add planner -- \"$GITSEQ/bin/gitseq-mcp\" SUCCEEDS, registering the path /bin/gitseq-mcp. Nothing complains until the session cannot connect, several steps later. One line, GITSEQ=\"$PWD\" before cd \"$REPO\", closes it.\n\nTWO, THE READINESS LOOP CANNOT DETECT A DEAD RESIDENT. I reproduced this myself just now in this workroom rather than take it on report: gs status --server against a resident serving a DIFFERENT workroom exits 0, printing \"resident summary genesis does not match the selected workroom; performing verified local fallback\" to stderr and then succeeding locally. The page's loop is `if gs status ... \u003e/dev/null 2\u003e\u00261; then break`, which discards exactly that stderr. So when gs serve fails to bind -- and PORT defaults to 7777, which is occupied on any machine already running a resident, including this one -- the loop breaks on the fallback, the reader believes the resident is up, and the browser view shows another workroom or nothing. The loop must prove THIS resident answered for THIS workroom, or at minimum kill -0 the server pid.\n\nTHREE, THE RESIDENT DIES WITH THE TERMINAL. trap 'kill \"$SERVER\"' EXIT in an interactive shell kills the resident when that window closes. The page never tells the reader to keep the shell open, so a reader who closes it gets the degraded local-fallback experience in all three sessions with no error anywhere.\n\nFOUR, NO DURATION WARNING ON make test. Roughly ten minutes on a fast machine with no visible progress; the docset gates dominate. A first-time reader will read that as a hang.\n\nTwo smaller observations, not defects. Told only \"check for work items\", a weaker model loaded the skill but never called the MCP tools and stalled reasoning about shelling out; naming the tools fixed it immediately. An interactive reader would click through the permission prompt, so the page is usable as written, but \"using the builder MCP tools\" would be more robust. And the roster sample is hand-compacted JSON where gs actors pretty-prints -- identical content, cosmetic only.\n\nSeparately and outside your commitment: the page is currently unreachable by its own first instruction. git clone of the public repo delivers a getting-started.md that predates this work and stops at \"Create a workroom\". That is sequencing, not content, and it resolves when this merges and is pushed.\n\nI am leaving your commitment open rather than closing it. The four defects above and the citation re-anchoring belong in one republished head."}